+(the second equality because $z_p^{-1}$ is central: $R(z_p^{-1})$ applied to $U'_pf=\sum_jf(\cdot\,u'_jg')$). It remains to identify $R(z_p^{-1})=\langle p\rangle_M$. Let $\gamma:=p^{-1}\in\Q^\times\subseteq B^\times$ (central) and
+$y:=\gamma^{-1}z_p^{-1}=p\,z_p^{-1}$, which is $1$ at $p$ and $p$ (a scalar) at every $\ell\ne p$. Then
+$f(xz_p^{-1})=f(x\gamma y)=f(\gamma xy)=f(xy)$ by left $B^\times$-invariance. Now $y_\ell=p\in\Z_\ell^\times$ for $\ell\ne p$, so $y\in\Uzero{Mp^s}$ with lower right entries $\equiv p$ at $\ell\mid M$ and $\equiv1$ at $p$; thus
+$y\in\delta_d\,\Uone{Mp^s}$ for $d\in(\Z/Mp^s)^\times$ with $d\equiv p\bmod M$, $d\equiv1\bmod p^s$, i.e.\ $d=\langle p\rangle_M$ (the diamond at the primes dividing $M$, trivial at $p$). By right $U_s$-invariance of $f$ (trivial $V$), $f(xy)=f(x\delta_d)=(\langle d\rangle f)(x)$, so $R(z_p^{-1})=\langle p\rangle_M$. Hence $U_p^*=\langle p\rangle_MU'_p$; $\langle p\rangle_M$ and $U'_p$ commute (the first is $R$ of a central element, or by (e)-type reasoning, since $\delta_d$ can be chosen diagonal at $p$ with $p$-component $1$). By the same reasoning, under $\nu$ (Prop.~\ref{prop:s01-adjoint}(b), $\nu\circ U_p=U_p^*\circ\nu$) the homological $U_p$ on $\Zp[S_s]$ becomes $\langle p\rangle_MU'_p$ on $\Zp^{S_s}$.
+Consistency with (b), (c): $\nu\pi_*=\mathrm{tr}\,\nu'$ and $\pi_*U_p=U_p\pi_*$ give $\mathrm{tr}\,U_p^*=U_p^*\mathrm{tr}$ (where $\nu$ is bijective, $p\ge5$), which is $\mathrm{tr}\,U'_p=U'_p\mathrm{tr}$ from (c) together with $\mathrm{tr}\,\langle p\rangle_M=\langle p\rangle_M\mathrm{tr}$ ($\delta_d\in\Uzero{Mp^{s+1}}$ normalises $U_s$ and $U_{s+1}$).
+
+\smallskip\noindent\emph{(e).} All operators in question are of the form $T_h=[UhU]$ with reps $h_j$, and the commutation of two such operators $T_g,T_h$ follows from the criteria of Prop.~\ref{prop:s01-relations}(c) below, whose proof uses only Def.~\ref{def:s01-hecke}: (i) if $g$ and $h$ have disjoint supports, then $U_p=T_{\varpi_p}$ ($g$ supported at $p$) commutes with $T_\ell$, $\ell\ne p$ (reps supported at $\ell$) and with $\Pi_\ell$, $S_\ell$ (normalising elements supported at $\ell\ne p$ or central); (ii) $\langle d\rangle$ is $T_\delta$ with $\delta$ normalising $U$ and, choosing $\delta_p$ diagonal, $\delta\varpi_p=\varpi_p\delta$, so criterion (ii) of (c) applies; (iii) right translation by $B_q^\times$, $q\ne p$, commutes with the reps $\varpi_j$ of $U_p$ (disjoint supports) and $U_p$ at level $g^{-1}Ug$ has the same reps $\varpi_j$ by (a). (For $\ell\nmid Mp$; for $\ell\mid M$ the same argument covers $U_\ell$.)
+\end{proof}
 
