+$(\delta_d\delta_{d'})_pf(x\delta_{dd'}u)$ with $u\in U_1$, which is $\delta_{dd',p}f(x\delta_{dd'})$ by the transformation law. Also $\langle d\rangle$ does not depend on the choice of $\delta_d$ (same computation), so we may choose $\delta_d$ diagonal, $=\mathrm{diag}(1,\tilde d)$ at each $\ell\mid Mp$ and $1$ elsewhere.
+
+\smallskip\noindent(a) As $z_\ell$ is central, $Uz_\ell U=z_\ell U$ is one coset, $z_{\ell,p}=1$, and $S_\ell f(x)=f(xz_\ell)$. Put $y:=\ell^{-1}z_\ell$, i.e.\ $\ell^{-1}$ (scalar) at every prime $\ne\ell$ and $1$ at $\ell$. Since $\ell\in\Q^\times\subseteq B^\times$ is central,
+$f(xz_\ell)=f(x\ell y)=f(\ell xy)=f(xy)$. For $\ell\nmid DMp$ we have $y\in\Uzero{Mp^s}$ with lower right entry $\equiv\ell^{-1}$ mod $Mp^s$, hence $y=\delta_{d}u$ with $d=\ell^{-1}$, $u\in\Uone{Mp^s}$, so
+$f(xy)=u_p^{-1}f(x\delta_d)=u_p^{-1}\delta_{d,p}^{-1}(\langle d\rangle f)(x)=y_p^{-1}(\langle\ell^{-1}\rangle f)(x)$. Now $y_p=\ell^{-1}I$ acts on $V_k$ by \eqref{eq:s01-Vk} as $P\mapsto P(\ell^{-1}X,\ell^{-1}Y)=\ell^{-(k-2)}P$, so $y_p^{-1}$ acts as $\ell^{k-2}$. Therefore $S_\ell=\ell^{k-2}\langle\ell^{-1}\rangle=\ell^{k-2}\langle\ell\rangle^{-1}$. At level $\Uzero{M}\cdot\GL_2(\Zp)$ (no $p$-level), $y\in U$ and $f(xy)=y_p^{-1}f(x)=\ell^{k-2}f(x)$.
+The same computation holds for $\ell\mid D$ (then $y_\ell=1$).
+
+\smallskip\noindent(b) Let $\ell\mid D$ and $\Pi_\ell$ a uniformiser of $B_\ell$, so $\Pi_\ell\cO_\ell=\cO_\ell\Pi_\ell$ is the maximal ideal and $\cO_\ell^\times$ is normalised by $\Pi_\ell$. Hence $U\Pi_\ell U=\Pi_\ell U$ is one coset and $\Pi_\ell f(x)=f(x\Pi_\ell)$ (no $V$-action as $\ell\ne p$). Because $\Pi_\ell^2\cO_\ell=\ell\cO_\ell$ we have $\Pi_\ell^2=\ell\varepsilon$ with $\varepsilon\in\cO_\ell^\times\subseteq U_\ell$,
